KQR row cache contention check: gets 1000000 slps 5482 limit 5000
KQR row cache hash chain marked hot: cid 7 hash 1cc87b7c
KQR: cid 10 bucket 31612 marked HOT (mutex sleeps > limit)
KQR (Kernel Query Row Cache)

KQR is the manager of the Row Cache in Oracle Database.

The Row Cache is part of the Shared Pool and stores Data Dictionary metadata, such as:
Users
Tables
Privileges
Objects
Synonyms

Oracle keeps this metadata in the Row Cache instead of going to the SYSTEM tables every time.
This makes dictionary lookups much faster.

Understanding the log message
gets = 1000000   ->  This means Oracle tried to access the Row Cache one million times.
slps = 5482      ->  This means there were 5482 mutex sleeps.
A mutex sleep happens when a session tries to access the cache but another session is holding the mutex, so it must wait.

limit = 5000     ->  This is the threshold for sleeps.
When the number of sleeps exceeds this limit, Oracle considers the hash bucket hot.

In our case
5482 > 5000

So there is contention on the Row Cache.

That is why Oracle reports:
row cache hash chain marked HOT
Meaning many sessions are competing for the same dictionary cache entry.

How to investigate
--Check Row Cache activity:
SELECT parameter,
       gets,
       getmisses,
       modifications
FROM v$rowcache
ORDER BY getmisses DESC;

This shows which dictionary cache entries are experiencing the most misses.

--Check mutex sleeps:
SELECT *
FROM v$mutex_sleep;

This helps identify where mutex contention is happening.

--Check SQL statements with heavy parsing:
SELECT sql_id,
       parse_calls,
       executions
FROM v$sqlarea
ORDER BY parse_calls DESC;

High parse calls may cause heavy data dictionary lookups, which increases Row Cache contention.

Typical reasons for this issue:
Too many hard parses
Applications constantly querying dictionary objects
Frequent DDL operations
Shared Pool pressure
